  "notes": [
    "Typedef targets are expanded by resolved identity, while declarations and generic binders preserve native constructor forwarding and tear-off inference.",
    "Native checks cover modern/legacy function aliases, nested generic functions, nullable aliases, partial/reordered generic arguments, const/type/constructor identity, SDK aliases, private constructors and static member access.",
    "Async alias acceptance uses the resolved return type, including Identity<Future<T>>; void-async remains outside the accepted subset.",
    "Changed alias targets and relocated underlying classes invalidate typed functions, globals and classes; incompatible functions remain module-local. Typedef removal does not delete a runtime class.",
    "Hierarchy aliases expand to real ancestors for closed-class checks. Parts/re-exports and same-name aliases retain library identity.",
    "Fresh baseline/patch Kernel inspections verify actual 3.0/3.4/3.12 versions.",
    "Initial tests had a wrong alias count and incorrectly expected static member access through aliases to fail. These assertions were corrected and static alias access was added to native positive coverage.",
    "The first multilang fixture omitted its part language override and was rejected by original analysis; corrected fixtures passed.",
    "Historical full native suite was not rerun; this report covers nineteen rebuilt groups."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Records and other unsupported alias targets, annotated declarations and unlinked SDK/native code remain outside the accepted subset.",
    "Full Flutter/mobile cold start, signing/rollback and physical-device performance remain incomplete. Alias relinking cost is not benchmarked."
